          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Integrated investigation: optimizing an interactive campus map",
          "content": "### Make a controlled performance hypothesis\nAn interactive campus map displays hundreds of buildings, trees, signs, and moving avatars. On a laptop it runs near 60 FPS, but on a midrange phone the camera occasionally freezes. A first hypothesis is that too many polygons are being drawn. Before simplifying geometry, collect a trace showing CPU and GPU work. If the CPU spends most of its time traversing thousands of scene nodes and issuing separate draw calls, instancing and transform caching may help. If the GPU spends most of its time shading overlapping foliage, geometry simplification alone may provide little benefit.\n\nBuild a repeatable camera path that passes through open areas and dense tree clusters. Record frame-time distributions at fixed viewport sizes and device pixel ratios. Inspect whether stalls coincide with entering new regions. If so, asset streaming or shader compilation may be the cause rather than steady-state rendering. Precompile common shader variants and prefetch nearby textures as a controlled experiment, while recording memory peaks. A performance fix that eliminates a stall by loading every asset at startup may simply move the problem into an unacceptable initial load time.\n\nIf tree rendering is CPU-bound, batch repeated meshes with instance transforms and use a conservative spatial index to cull invisible groups. Validate culling by temporarily disabling it and comparing the resulting images. Any missing visible tree is a correctness failure even if performance improves. If foliage is fragment-bound, investigate alpha-tested leaves, overdraw, and resolution. Test a lower render scale and observe whether GPU time changes as predicted. A strong reduction suggests a pixel-related bottleneck; a small change suggests another limiting stage.\n\nThe map must also be useful as a learning tool. Preserve readable labels when lowering visual quality. Keep important accessibility features such as keyboard navigation and text alternatives for interactive controls. Provide a quality mode that prioritizes clear building outlines and signs over cinematic reflections. Report final performance with the test hardware, scene revision, resolution, and percentile frame times. The investigation should conclude with measured causes and documented tradeoffs, not a claim that one universal optimization makes all scenes faster."
