          "secNumber": "8.5",
          "heading": "Integrated investigation: a reproducible climate visualization",
          "content": "### Build a chain of evidence from measurements to pixels\nA research group wants to publish an interactive map showing a simulated regional temperature anomaly over several decades. The raw dataset contains grid cells with latitude, longitude, time, and temperature differences relative to a defined reference period. The visualization team must choose a projection, interpolation method, color palette, time controls, and uncertainty display. Before designing the appearance, verify units and metadata. An anomaly in degrees Celsius has the same increment size as an anomaly in kelvin, but the reference period determines the meaning of zero. A label reading simply \u201ctemperature\u201d would be misleading.\n\nUse a diverging color palette centered on zero anomaly and document the color range. If the range changes automatically for each year, the same color can represent different values at different times, making visual comparison difficult. A fixed range supports cross-year comparison but may clip rare extremes. A good interface can show clipped values explicitly and provide a numerical tooltip. Geographic projections also distort area, distance, or shape; when comparing area-weighted statistics, use the appropriate cell areas rather than counting map pixels. A map projection is a visualization transform, not a license to change the underlying physical weighting.\n\nInterpolation fills gaps between grid samples. Bilinear interpolation can produce a smooth map, but it does not create new measurements or guarantee that small-scale features are physically real. Mask regions with missing observations or uncertain model coverage. If the animation blends between annual states, state that intermediate frames are a visual interpolation unless the underlying model provides higher temporal resolution. A slider that looks smooth may otherwise imply month-by-month predictions that the data do not support.\n\nFor performance, generate appropriate levels of detail for different zoom scales and cache derived map tiles. Preserve numerical precision in the data layer even if the display uses compressed colors. A mobile viewer may render fewer geographic details, but its numeric readouts and legends should remain accurate. Test extreme values, missing cells, map boundaries, and date transitions. A failure at the international date line or a longitude wrap can create a visually dramatic seam unrelated to climate. Validate the output against a small independent table of known grid values and against the original dataset at sampled locations.\n\nFinally publish a reproducibility record: data source and version, reference period, spatial projection, interpolation method, palette limits, code revision, and known uncertainty. Provide a static alternative for users whose devices cannot run the interactive map. The scientific contribution is not the animation alone; it is the transparent connection from data through transformations to a claim a reader can evaluate. This standard should guide any graphics product that influences education, engineering, or public decisions."
